---

<Template metaTitle="Understanding Numbers" resources={mathLinks}>
  
  <PageHeader 
    title="Numbers and Set theory" 
    description="What are numbers and how do they work?"
  />

  <SubTopic title="Number Sets.">
    <p>
        Set theory is a branch of mathematics that studies sets, which can be described as a collection of objects or types of numbers.
    </p>
    <h3>What Types of numbers are there?</h3>
    <p>
        There are 7 types of number sets starting with Natural numbers. Denoted by the symbol: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{N}"/>, Whole Numbers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{W}"/>, Integers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{Z}"/>
        Rational Numbers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{Q}"/>, Irrational Numbers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{P}"/>, Real Numbers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{R}"/>, and Complex Numbers: <Linemath formula="\\mathbb{C}"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The <strong>Natural Numbers</strong> consist of numbers you would use for counting. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5... ect ect. We call them natural because they are the numbers we can use to represent a real physical number of 
        things you can have.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Whole Numbers</strong> include the entire set of Natural Numbers and 0, so 0, 1, 2, 3, 4... ect ect. </p>
    <p><strong>Integers</strong> include the entire set of whole numbers and their negative counterparts. so -2, -1, 0, 1, 2... ect ect. </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Rational Numbers</strong> are all numbers that can be expressed as a fraction. So <Linemath formula="\\frac{1}{2}"/> = <Linemath formula=".5"/>, <Linemath formula="\\frac{-5}{32}"/> = <Linemath formula="-0.15625"/>, 
        and <Linemath formula="\\frac{131}{28564}"/> = <Linemath formula="0.00458619241"/> are all rational numbers. This set also includes all Integers as any integer n can be expressed as a fraction <Linemath formula="\\frac{n}{1}"/>. 
        If you start with any integer n in the numerator and divide it by any integer d in the denominator, <Linemath formula="\\frac{n}{d}"/>, the result will be a rational number expressed either as a fraction or a decimal. 
        So any fraction can be expressed as a decimal of finite length or as a decimal that has a repeating pattern, such as <Linemath formula="\\frac{1}{3}"/> = <Linemath formula="0.33\\overline{3}"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Up to this point every new set of numbers has contained inside of it the entire previous set of numbers. Rational Numbers contain every Integer, Integers contain every Whole number, and Whole numbers contain every 
        Natural Number. That is not the case with the next set.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Irrational Numbers</strong> Contain every number that cannot be represented as a fraction. So numbers like <Linemath formula="\\pi"/>, <Linemath formula="e"/>, <Linemath formula="\\sqrt{2}"/> or any
        square root where the radicand is not a perfect square. The radicand is the number we are taking the root of, in the above example the radicand is 2. So for any root  <Linemath formula="\\sqrt{n}"/> where n is not 
        equal to some number squared, the root will be an irrational number. Because an Irrational Number cannot be expressed as a fraction they must be approximated as a decimal. The problem with this is that because they 
        are not rational they have a infinite number of decimal places, <Linemath formula="\\pi"/> is famous for having been calculated to the trillions of decimal places with the world record at the time of writing being 
        314,000,000,000,000 digits. Because of this you can only approximate the real value of a Irrational Number, and luckily you can have incredibly accurate approximations, and be very precise with them for example nasa 
        only uses 15 decimal places of pi for its most accurate calculations. I encourage you to read their article linked at the bottom of this page, it is highly informative. 
    </p>
    <p>
        The Set of Irrational Numbers does not include any of the set of Rational Numbers. 
    </p>

  </SubTopic>

</Template>